      "checklist_item": {
        "check_id": "W7B8:W7B8:CHK-0163",
        "property_id": "PROP-W5B14-30",
        "title": "Falsification Test: Can the inverse FFT normalization factor 1/N be bypassed or computed incorrectly, causing ifft(fft(p)) != p?",
        "severity": "High",
        "mindset": "Formal Verification Engineer",
        "is_boundary_check": false,
        "reachability": {
          "classification": "internal-only",
          "entry_points": [
            "Internal"
          ],
          "attacker_controlled": false,
          "validation_layers": [],
          "bug_bounty_scope": "in-scope",
          "notes": "The inverse FFT is used in polynomial_eval_to_coeff and recover_polynomialcoeff. Incorrect normalization produces wrong coefficients."
        },
        "test_procedure": "1. Locate the fft_field function (SG-004) and its inverse FFT implementation in the codebase.\n2. Identify the normalization step: inverse FFT MUST multiply by 1/N where N is the array length.\n3. CRITICAL: Verify the normalization factor is computed as the modular multiplicative inverse of N in the field, not integer division.\n4. Design falsification tests for the roundtrip invariant ifft(fft(p)) == p:\n   (a) Test with the zero polynomial (all zero coefficients)\n   (b) Test with the one polynomial (constant term = 1, rest zeros)\n   (c) Test with a polynomial where all coefficients = 1\n   (d) Test with random coefficient values\n   (e) Test with maximum field element values (modulus - 1)\n   (f) Test with polynomial degree = N-1 (maximum degree)\n5. Verify roots_of_unity order reversal: inverse FFT MUST reverse the roots_of_unity order (except the first element).\n   (a) For forward FFT: use roots_of_unity as [1, w, w^2, ..., w^(N-1)]\n   (b) For inverse FFT: use roots_of_unity as [1, w^(N-1), w^(N-2), ..., w^1]\n   (c) Verify the implementation correctly swaps or reverses the roots.\n6. Test normalization edge cases:\n   (a) N = 1: normalization factor should be 1 (1/1 = 1)\n   (b) N = 2: verify 1/2 mod field_modulus is correctly computed\n   (c) N = 4096 (typical EIP-4844 value): verify 1/4096 mod field_modulus\n   (d) N = field_modulus - 1: edge case for modular inverse\n7. Trace the normalization implementation:\n   (a) Check if normalization is applied element-wise after butterfly operations\n   (b) Verify normalization is not accidentally applied in forward FFT\n   (c) Check for off-by-one errors in normalization loop\n8. Test polynomial_eval_to_coeff and recover_polynomialcoeff:\n   (a) These functions use inverse FFT - verify they receive correctly normalized output\n   (b) Test with known polynomial coefficients, compute evaluations, then recover coefficients\n   (c) Verify recovered coefficients exactly match original coefficients\n9. Check for precision/rounding issues: field arithmetic should be exact with no floating point\n10. Verify modular inverse computation:\n   (a) Check the algorithm used (extended Euclidean algorithm, Fermat's little theorem, etc.)\n   (b) Verify it handles edge cases correctly\n   (c) Confirm result satisfies: (N * (1/N)) mod field_modulus == 1\n11. Cross-reference with KZG commitment verification:\n   (a) Incorrect FFT normalization would produce wrong polynomial coefficients\n   (b) This would cause commitment mismatches and proof verification failures\n12. Impact analysis: if normalization is wrong by any constant factor, all polynomial coefficient computations are corrupted, breaking proofs and data recovery.\n13. Property-based testing: generate random polynomials and verify ifft(fft(p)) == p holds for all.\n14. Compare with reference implementation: if available, compare FFT outputs against a known-correct implementation.\n15. Verify the invariant SG-004:INV-001 is enforced across all code paths that use fft_field for inverse FFT.",
        "bug_class": "Mathematical Invariant Violation / Cryptographic Correctness",
        "risk_category": "Tampering",
        "notes": "Source: PROP-W5B14-30, Invariant: SG-004:INV-001, Primary Element: SG-004:fft_field, Covered Nodes: actor-validator-node. The inverse FFT normalization factor 1/N is critical for polynomial coefficient recovery. If normalization is missing, incorrect, or applied inconsistently, all polynomial operations downstream are corrupted. This affects polynomial_eval_to_coeff and recover_polynomialcoeff, which are essential for cell recovery and KZG proof generation. The roots_of_unity order reversal (except first element) is equally critical - incorrect ordering produces garbage outputs. While classified as internal-only, corruption propagates to all cryptographic proofs. The test should attempt to FALSIFY the roundtrip invariant by finding edge cases where ifft(fft(p)) != p.",
